-- DICT-SEMANTICS §1 + §4 `gen-sig`: the POSITIVE half of s1-nary-predicate-enforced.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): §4 `gen-sig` makes the ascribed `Ix a i` the
-- binding's principal context, so `twoParam` abstracts ONE dictionary parameter
-- for the joint 2-ary predicate and its displayed scheme keeps `Ix a b =>`. At
-- the call `twoParam 1 2` the goal is `Ix Int Int`, matched uniquely by the one
-- instance, so `inst` fires and `ix a i = a + i` gives 1 + 2 = 3.
--
-- OBSERVED: check ACCEPT, scheme `twoParam : Ix a b => a -> b -> Int`,
-- run/build both print 3. CONFORMANT.
--
-- The SCHEME half matters independently of the value: #607's discriminating
-- probe was `check --types` showing whether the constraint survives at all
-- (it printed `a -> b -> Int`, constraint GONE, while still exiting 0). The
-- gate asserts the scheme line, not just the verdict, for exactly that reason.
interface Ix a i where
  ix : a -> i -> Int

impl Ix Int Int where
  ix a i = a + i

twoParam : Ix a i => a -> i -> Int
twoParam a i = ix a i

main = println (twoParam (1 : Int) (2 : Int))
